Here's how to enable internet access for your private EC2 instance using an Amazon Linux 2023 NAT instance.

1. Create Public Subnet & Internet Gateway
Create Public Subnet:

Go to VPC Console > Subnets > Create subnet.

Select datacenter-priv-vpc.

Subnet name: datacenter-pub-subnet.

Choose an Availability Zone (matching or compatible with your private subnet).

Specify a CIDR block (e.g., 10.0.1.0/24, assuming your VPC uses 10.0.0.0/16).

Internet Gateway & Route Table:

Ensure an Internet Gateway (IGW) is attached to datacenter-priv-vpc (create and attach one if it doesn't exist).

Create a public route table (or use the existing public one), add a route:

Destination: 0.0.0.0/0

Target: Internet Gateway (igw-xxxx)

Associate this route table with datacenter-pub-subnet.

Enable Auto-assign public IPv4 address on datacenter-pub-subnet.

2. Configure Security Group for NAT Instance
Go to EC2 Console > Security Groups > Create security group.

Name: datacenter-nat-sg

VPC: datacenter-priv-vpc

Inbound Rules:

HTTP (80) & HTTPS (443) (or All Traffic for testing) from the CIDR of datacenter-priv-subnet (e.g., 10.0.2.0/24).

Outbound Rules:

All Traffic to 0.0.0.0/0.

3. Launch & Configure NAT Instance
Launch Instance:

Name: datacenter-nat-instance

AMI: Amazon Linux 2023

Network settings:

VPC: datacenter-priv-vpc

Subnet: datacenter-pub-subnet

Auto-assign Public IP: Enable

Security Group: Select datacenter-nat-sg

Disable Source/Destination Check:

Select datacenter-nat-instance > Actions > Networking > Change source/destination check.

Check Stop and save changes.

IMPORTANT: PRIVATE ROUTE TABLE > EDIT INBOUND > 0.0.0.0/0 >INSTANCE>NAT (ASSOCIATE NAT INSTANCE TO THE PRIVATE ROUTE TABLE VIA 0.0.0.0/0)

Configure NAT Routing inside the Instance:
Connect via SSH or EC2 Instance Connect to datacenter-nat-instance and run:

Bash
# Update system and install iptables services
sudo dnf install -y iptables-services

# Enable IPv4 forwarding in kernel
echo "net.ipv4.ip_forward=1" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.d/custom-ip-forward.conf
sudo sysctl -p /etc/sysctl.d/custom-ip-forward.conf

# Identify your primary network interface (usually eth0)
IFACE=$(ip route | grep default | awk '{print $5}')

# Configure IP masquerading via iptables
sudo iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -o $IFACE -j MASQUERADE
sudo iptables -A FORWARD -i $IFACE -m state --state RELATED,ESTABLISHED -j ACCEPT
sudo iptables -A FORWARD -i eth+ -j ACCEPT

# Save iptables rules and enable service on boot
sudo service iptables save
sudo systemctl enable --now iptables
4. Update Private Subnet Route Table
Go to VPC Console > Route Tables.

Select the route table associated with datacenter-priv-subnet.

Go to Routes > Edit routes.

Add a new route:

Destination: 0.0.0.0/0

Target: Instance > Select datacenter-nat-instance.

Save changes.

5. Verification
Wait 1–2 minutes for the cron job on datacenter-priv-ec2 to execute.

Go to S3 Console > Buckets > datacenter-nat-5135.

Verify that datacenter-test.txt appears in the bucket list.
